    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d624">

                <pb facs="0554" n="542" sameAs="frus:frus1940v05/pg542"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.6354/695</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Chief of the Division of the
                        American Republics</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Duggan</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Adviser on International Economic Affairs</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Feis</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-09-27">September 27, 1940</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dr. Feis</hi>: I have read with interest your
                    memorandum of conversation with Sir Owen Chalkley on the Bolivian tin situation.
                    I was somewhat amazed at his bland disregard of the Bolivian needs. His
                    statement that the purchase of Straits’ tin would require no dollar exchange
                    indicates that Great Britain hopes to be able to purchase Bolivian tin without
                    exchange. Just how does he expect Bolivia is going to live?</p>
                <p>I agree that probably Patiño is behind this maneuver but I frankly do not find it
                    at all relishing that the British should be so completely impervious to the
                    needs of Bolivia.<anchor n="21" xml:id="d624-f1"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Laurence Duggan</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d624-f1" n="21" xml:id="d624-f1-content">
                <p>The following notation appears at the bottom of this memorandum: “Mr. Duggan:
                    Pinsent [Mr. G. H. S. Pinsent, Financial Counselor of the British Embassy] came
                    to see me later on the same subject &amp; I brought out your point more clearly.
                    H[erbert] F[eis].”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
